## Main Product Areas and Capabilities

### Transactional Sending: Email and SMS

SendPro sends transactional emails and SMS messages triggered by system events.

Supported sending methods:

- API
- SMTP

Supported channels:

- Email
- SMS

Typical system events:

- Orders
- Logins
- Password resets
- Account changes
- Status changes
- Delivery updates
- Appointment reminders
- Authentication codes
- Security alerts
- Service notifications

Design implication:

- Sending is system-triggered, not audience-broadcast marketing.
- Speed, reliability, monitoring, and troubleshooting matter more than campaign design.
- Email and SMS may be used in different flows depending on urgency and use case.

---

### Sources

A source represents a sending system connected to SendPro.

Examples of sources:

- Webshop
- CRM system
- SaaS application
- Internal platform
- Customer portal
- Service system
- Authentication system

Sources help organisations:

- Separate systems
- Monitor delivery per system
- Troubleshoot issues
- Manage flows centrally
- Understand which application sent which message

Design implication:

- Source is a core SendPro concept.
- Multi-system environments should be organised around sources.
- Reports and troubleshooting should allow filtering by source.
- Avoid treating all transactional messages as coming from one generic sender.

---

### Transactional Flows

Transactional flows define how system-triggered messages are managed.

Flows may determine:

- Which channel is used
- Which template is applied
- Which source triggers the message
- Which language or brand applies
- Which message type is being sent
- How the message is structured
- How reporting is separated

Flow examples:

- Order confirmation email
- Shipping update email
- Password reset email
- Login code SMS
- Service notification
- Appointment reminder
- Return update
- Security alert

Design implication:

- Flows are structured around system events and message purpose.
- Users need clarity about source, trigger, channel, template, and status.
- Do not design flows as marketing journeys or nurture campaigns.

---

### Templates and Message Content

SendPro supports central template management for transactional messages.

Email template capabilities:

- HTML templates
- Freemarker template engine
- Dynamic content
- Personalisation from system data
- Conditional content
- Nested loops
- Reuse of system-supplied message content
- Reusable headers
- Reusable footers
- Content blocks
- Multilingual scenarios
- Multi-brand scenarios
- Visual Quick editor for simpler templates

SMS content capabilities:

- SMS content per use case
- SMS content per source
- Short, time-critical message structure

Design implication:

- Templates can range from simple to highly complex.
- Some users may edit text and variables in the Quick editor; others may manage HTML and Freemarker.
- Template logic is technical but should be understandable enough for content and operations users.
- Avoid assuming templates are edited only by developers.

---

### Freemarker Template Engine

SendPro templates can use Freemarker for dynamic and personalised transactional content.

Freemarker supports:

- Variables
- Customer data
- Order data
- Conditional content
- Different content per situation
- Nested loops
- Dynamic content structures
- Reuse of data from source systems

Design implication:

- Advanced templates may need code-like logic.
- Designs should help users understand variables, conditions, loops, previews, and test data.
- Errors in template logic can affect critical messages, so validation and preview are important.

---

### Quick Editor

The visual Quick editor supports less complex template changes.

Users can adjust:

- Text
- Images
- Variables
- Simple content fields

Design implication:

- Quick editor is useful for non-technical users.
- It should not expose unnecessary HTML complexity.
- The design should make clear when a user is editing simple content versus advanced template logic.

---

### Reporting and Insight

SendPro provides delivery and performance reporting for transactional email and SMS.

Reporting may show:

- Which messages were sent
- When messages were sent
- Which source sent the message
- Which message type was used
- Delivery status
- Delivery errors
- Performance data
- Open information where available
- Reporting by source
- Reporting by message type

Design implication:

- Reporting should support monitoring and troubleshooting.
- Users often need to answer: “Was this critical message sent and delivered?”
- Reports should separate source and message type.
- Avoid marketing-style campaign analytics as the default mental model.

---

### Connectivity and Webhooks

SendPro integrates with systems through API, SMTP, and webhooks.

Connectivity capabilities:

- API for direct application or platform connection
- SMTP for existing systems that already send email through SMTP
- Webhooks for real-time updates
- Status feedback to internal platforms
- Monitoring and troubleshooting integration

Design implication:

- Developers need clear configuration and status feedback.
- Webhooks are used to push message events back to internal systems.
- Designs should distinguish sending methods from event feedback methods.

---

### Deliverability and Infrastructure

SendPro is built for reliable transactional delivery.

Capabilities include:

- Advanced deliverability controls
- Deliverability monitoring
- High-performance infrastructure
- Scalable infrastructure for high sending volumes
- Predictable delivery during peak loads
- Enterprise-grade availability
- Separate transactional sending from marketing mail

Design implication:

- Deliverability is core product value.
- Users need confidence that critical messages are fast, reliable, and monitored.
- Avoid hiding deliverability or treating it as a secondary analytics feature.

---

### SLA and Enterprise Capabilities

SendPro supports SLA and enterprise-grade requirements.

SLA and enterprise concepts:

- Standard SLA
- Premium SLA
- Platform availability
- Performance
- Support agreements
- Scalable infrastructure
- High sending volumes
- Advanced deliverability monitoring
- Multiple sources
- Central control
- API and webhook flexibility
- 24/7 emergency line for core feature outages

Design implication:

- Enterprise features should support business-critical reliability.
- SLA information should be understandable and tied to availability, performance, and support.
- Avoid treating emergency support or SLA as generic account settings only.

