## Feature Design Guidance

### Design for critical system communication, not marketing campaigns

SendPro is for transactional messages triggered by systems.

Good designs should support:

- Message type
- Source
- Trigger
- Flow
- Template
- Delivery status
- Error state
- Webhook feedback
- SLA and reliability context

Avoid:

- Marketing campaign language
- Newsletter assumptions
- Audience-building flows
- Promotional campaign analytics as the primary pattern

---

### Make source separation clear

Multiple systems may send messages through one SendPro environment.

Good designs should show:

- Which source sent a message
- Which flows belong to a source
- Which message types are active
- Delivery performance per source
- Integration status per source

Avoid merging all sending systems into one indistinct message stream.

---

### Make delivery status and troubleshooting actionable

Users often need to know whether a critical message arrived.

Good designs should support:

- Delivery status lookup
- Error details
- Message timeline
- Source filter
- Message type filter
- Retry or corrective actions where applicable
- Webhook feedback state

Avoid reports that do not help users troubleshoot.

---

### Support both technical and non-technical users

Developers configure APIs, SMTP, webhooks, and complex templates. Content or operations users may manage templates and reports.

Good designs should separate:

- Developer setup
- Source configuration
- Template editing
- Quick editor
- Delivery reporting
- Operational troubleshooting

Avoid exposing unnecessary code complexity to users who only need content edits or delivery insight.

---

### Treat templates as operationally important

Transactional templates affect critical customer communications.

Good designs should support:

- Preview with sample data
- Variable validation
- Freemarker validation
- Template usage visibility
- Version awareness where relevant
- Quick editor for simple changes
- Advanced editing for complex templates

Avoid allowing blind template edits without preview or validation.

---

### Make flows channel-aware

SendPro supports both email and SMS.

Good designs should show:

- Which channel a flow uses
- When SMS is selected instead of email
- Which message type belongs to which channel
- Channel-specific content limits or expectations

Avoid assuming every transactional message is email.

---

### Make SLA and enterprise context practical

Enterprise customers care about availability, performance, support, and emergency processes.

Good designs should show:

- SLA level
- Availability or performance context
- Support options
- Emergency line information where relevant
- High-volume or source configuration context

Avoid hiding SLA information in generic account settings when the feature concerns reliability.

---

## Important Assumptions to Preserve

Preserve these assumptions when generating product concepts, UX flows, screens, or feature ideas:

- Spotler SendPro is a transactional messaging platform, not an email marketing platform.
- Transactional messages are system-triggered and sent to specific individuals.
- Email and SMS are both supported.
- API and SMTP are sending methods.
- Webhooks provide real-time event feedback.
- Sources represent connected sending systems.
- Flows represent structured transactional scenarios.
- Templates are centrally managed and can be simple or advanced.
- HTML and Freemarker are important for dynamic email templates.
- Quick editor supports simpler template changes.
- Delivery status, reporting, and troubleshooting are core product value.
- SendPro separates transactional sending from marketing mail.
- Deliverability, reliability, speed, and scalability are central.
- SLA and enterprise capabilities matter for business-critical use cases.
- Multiple systems can send through one SendPro environment.
- Critical messages such as password resets, order confirmations, and authentication codes must be fast and reliable.

---

## Things Figma Make Should Avoid

Avoid these mistakes when designing Spotler SendPro features:

- Do not treat SendPro as a newsletter or marketing campaign platform.
- Do not design audience segmentation or promotional campaign flows unless explicitly requested.
- Do not ignore sources; they are central to multi-system sending.
- Do not ignore flows; they structure transactional scenarios.
- Do not treat webhooks as a sending method.
- Do not assume all messages are email; SMS is also supported.
- Do not hide delivery status, errors, or troubleshooting details.
- Do not treat templates as simple static content only.
- Do not ignore Freemarker, variables, conditions, or dynamic content where relevant.
- Do not force all users into code editing when the Quick editor is appropriate.
- Do not ignore SLA, infrastructure, or deliverability when designing enterprise or reliability features.
- Do not replace product terminology with vague terms like “items”, “records”, or “campaigns”.
- Do not overfocus on visual styling; that belongs in the Design System Skill.

---

## Edge Cases and Product Logic to Consider

### Message triggered but not delivered

A system may successfully trigger a message, but delivery may fail. Designs should distinguish trigger success from delivery success.

### Webhook failed

A message may be delivered, but the webhook update back to the internal system may fail. Designs should show webhook status separately.

### Source misconfiguration

A source may be connected incorrectly, causing delivery or authentication issues.

### Template variable missing

A template may reference data that the source system did not provide. Designs should show validation or fallback behaviour.

### Freemarker logic error

Advanced template logic can fail because of incorrect conditions, loops, or variables. Preview and validation are important.

### SMS selected for time-critical flow

An authentication or security flow may use SMS because email is slower or less visible. Designs should account for channel-specific behaviour.

### Multiple sources send similar message types

Several systems may send password resets or notifications. Reporting should separate by source.

### Peak sending volume

A webshop or platform may generate many messages during peak periods. Designs should show performance and delivery reliability.

### Multilingual or multi-brand template selection

Flows may apply different templates based on language or brand. Designs should make this logic understandable.

### Source-supplied message content reused

A source system may provide existing content that SendPro enriches with headers, footers, or content blocks. Designs should support this without forcing source-system changes.

### User without technical knowledge edits content

Quick editor should let non-technical users safely edit simpler template content without touching HTML or Freemarker.

### Business-critical outage

Enterprise users may need emergency support or SLA-related information during a core feature outage.

---

## Relationship to the Separate Design System Skill

This Skill provides product context only.

Use this Skill for:

- Product behaviour
- Workflows
- Transactional messaging terminology
- User roles
- Source logic
- Flow logic
- API, SMTP, and webhook context
- Template and Freemarker logic
- Quick editor context
- Delivery reporting and troubleshooting
- SLA and enterprise reliability context
- Feature-specific product assumptions

Do not use this Skill for:

- Component styling
- Typography
- Colour usage
- Layout systems
- Spacing
- Icons
- Button styles
- Form styling
- Navigation visual patterns
- Design tokens

For those topics, use the separate Spotler Design System Skill or attached Design System documentation.

When both Skills are available:

- Use this Skill to decide what the feature should do and how it should behave.
- Use the Design System Skill to decide how the feature should look and be constructed visually.

---

## Final Checklist for Figma Make

Before generating or redesigning a Spotler SendPro feature, check:

- Does the design use SendPro terminology?
- Is the relevant user role clear?
- Is the feature grounded in transactional email, transactional SMS, sources, flows, templates, API, SMTP, webhooks, delivery reporting, deliverability, troubleshooting, or SLA?
- Does the design avoid marketing campaign or newsletter assumptions?
- Does it distinguish source, flow, trigger, template, channel, and delivery status?
- Does it make delivery status and errors actionable?
- Does it account for both email and SMS where relevant?
- Does it distinguish API and SMTP from webhooks?
- Does it support both developers and non-technical content or operations users?
- Does template editing include preview, variables, and validation where relevant?
- Does it preserve Freemarker and dynamic content logic where relevant?
- Does it account for enterprise reliability and SLA when relevant?
- Does it avoid visual styling decisions that belong to the Design System Skill?

